//? Title: Network in Network: Shared Patch MLPs and Class Maps
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
One affine filter followed by ReLU cannot express every useful pattern in an image patch.
Network in Network replaces that local map with a shared multilayer perceptron. We verify
pointwise XOR and explicit patch-MLP equivalence, then train the full three-block topology
at compact widths using visible tensor Adam updates on original CIFAR-10 images.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/network_in_network.cpp`.

> The method

An MLPconv block applies a spatial convolution, then two 1×1 convolutions, with ReLU after
each layer. If a local patch is p, the map is ReLU(ReLU(ReLU(pW0+b0)W1+b1)W2+b2).
Sharing these matrices over locations is exactly a sliding MLP; a 1×1 convolution mixes channels
without adding spatial support. For x1,x2 in {0,1}, the two-ReLU construction is

$$
f(x)=\operatorname{ReLU}(x_1+x_2)-2\operatorname{ReLU}(x_1+x_2-1).
$$
\alttext(Two rectified sums compute XOR: zero on equal-input corners and one otherwise.)

No affine separator labels the two diagonal XOR pairs differently: adding each pair's
inequalities yields a contradiction. Shared nonlinear hidden features remove that restriction.
The CIFAR topology uses widths 192/160/96, then 192/192/192, then 192/192/10, with spatial
kernels 5/5/3. SAME max-pooling and dropout follow the first two blocks. The final ReLU maps
average directly into ten logits. Full widths learn 966986 scalars. A block costs
O(NHW(k² Cin A+AB+BC)), with A/B/C its three widths.

The report's thirty-epoch fit uses width divisor eight, 128 fitting/64 validation rows from
binary batch one and 64 official test rows. It keeps dropout and every block but uses Adam,
He initialization and [-1,1] pixels; it does not reproduce original contrast normalization,
ZCA preprocessing or the paper's training results. Class maps are scores, not masks or
spatial class probabilities. Cached CIFAR-10 is required; no download runs here.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/network_in_network_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/network_in_network_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/network_in_network_concept.tikz > build/network_in_network_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/network_in_network_concept.svg build/network_in_network_concept.dvi > build/network_in_network_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
