> Native confusion matrices

These matrices use the held-out predictions from the executed program above.
The native `ConfusionMatrix` SVG API labels exact counts with true classes
running from top to bottom and predicted classes from left to right.

\image(figures/network_in_network_confusion_api_0.svg)
\caption(Held-out classification confusion counts, evaluation 1. Exact cell counts are printed in the image.)
\alttext(Integer prediction counts with true classes as rows and predicted classes as columns.)


> Conclusion

The pointwise XOR and explicit patch comparison establish why MLPconv differs from a
single linear filter. Full-width shape/count checks and compact explicit training serve
different purposes; the short CIFAR demonstration establishes neither benchmark accuracy
nor an interpretation of class maps as ground-truth segmentation.

> References

- [Lin, Chen and Yan (2014), Network In Network](https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.4400).
- [CIFAR-10 binary dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html).
